  signal, the one every child on the
  island had learned before they learned
  to tie their shoes.

  There was nobody on the landing. Then
  a kitchen door opened in one of the
  cottages above it, and a woman in a
  bathrobe came out holding a mug and
  stared. Then she waved, a big slow
  wave over her head, and kept waving
  until they were past.

  Next was Cobb's Wharf, at 7:45. Walt
  sounded the horn again. This time a
  man came out of the chandlery, and
  then the girl who worked the bait
  counter, and then two more people from
  the café, still in their aprons.

  By the time they reached the Narrows
  landing, word had gotten ahead of
  them. People phone each other on an
  island. Thirty or forty of them stood
  along the seawall, and somebody had
  brought a handbell from the primary
  school, and somebody else had a
  saucepan and a wooden spoon. When Walt
  sounded the signal, the whole seawall
  answered with a racket of clanging
  and cheering that carried right across
  the water.

  Teo stood in the wheelhouse door with

